# SULFNBK.EXE hoax (2001). A chain email told people a virus hid as a Windows file called SULFNBK.EXE, and to delete it.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee closes IT's health-check tickets, closes one real ticket (a backup check), then meets a
# chain email that begs Dana to delete a genuine Windows tool and pass the warning on. There is no malware: the quest is about not
# overreacting. The office, the people and the intranet are made up.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | In 2001 a chain email warned that a virus called SULFNBK.EXE was hiding on your computer and would strike on 1 June. It told people to find the file, delete it and warn everyone they knew. Many people did, and the warning travelled round the world by itself.
  | There was no virus. SULFNBK.EXE was a real Windows utility, a small tool that restores long file names. The only harm was the panic, the deleted file and the flood of forwards. The office, the people and the intranet here are made up.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office administrator at Northwind Traders. Marcus Lee in IT keeps a short daily list of computer health-check tickets on the intranet for you to close: check a backup, confirm the updates. Each one comes to you by email from IT.
  | It is Monday morning. You have closed 4 of today's 6 tickets. Two to go.
  | Your intranet details are in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 4 6 Health-check tickets closed today
spotted reasons not to panic

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/intranet-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Northwind Intranet: IT tickets
  | Staff ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Cedar-2026!

# The file the email names. It is a genuine, signed Windows tool that was always there, so nothing flags it.
program sulfnbk SULFNBK.EXE
  publisher Halyard Software Corporation
  signed yes
  description Restores long file names (a Windows utility)
view sulfnbk-confirm message
  title Confirm File Delete
  icon question
  | Are you sure you want to delete 'SULFNBK.EXE'?
  button Yes -> done primary
  button No -> close
folder Home/Windows
folder Home/Windows/Command
file Home/Windows/Command/SULFNBK.EXE
  program sulfnbk
  size 29696

# The IT tickets: a sign-in page, and the first ticket behind it.
page intranet-login intranet.northwind.example/tickets/sign-in
  title Northwind Intranet: sign in
  brand Northwind Intranet
  favorite
  heading Sign in to IT tickets
  | Welcome back. Sign in to see your health-check tickets.
  field Staff ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Cedar-2026!
  button Sign in
page ticket5-done intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t5/closed
  title Ticket 5 closed
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Ticket 5 closed
  | Backup check recorded for NW-DANA-LT. Thank you, Dana.
page ticket5-held intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t5/held
  title Ticket 5 on hold
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Ticket 5 on hold
  | Marcus is told, and will look at the backup.
page ticket-5 intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t5
  title Ticket 5 of 6: check last night's backup
  brand Northwind Intranet
  requires intranet-login
  heading Ticket 5 of 6: check last night's backup
  | IT asks you to confirm that your computer's backup ran.
  section Request
  row Computer = NW-DANA-LT (yours)
  row Backup = Last night, 11:40pm
  row Result = Finished, 0 files skipped
  row Written by = Marcus Lee, IT
  row Checked by = Grace Kim, IT manager
  button Confirm the backup and close the ticket -> ticket5-done
  button Hold and ask IT -> ticket5-held

mail mail-t5
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Ticket 5: check last night's backup
  sent 2026-11-02 08:38
  link ticket-5
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Ticket 5 is a quick one: a look at last night's backup on your computer. The ticket says what ran and when.
  |
  | The link opens it on the intranet.
  |
  | Marcus, IT

step Ticket 5 of 6
  look Mail: Marcus's message with the ticket link
  | A new ticket has come in from Marcus, in IT. This one is routine. Do it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Ticket 5: check last night's backup

step The link goes to the intranet
  look the link under Marcus's message
  when mail mail-t5
  | The email links to the company's own address, intranet.northwind.example, for a ticket you expect. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-t5

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Staff ID and Password, as on every visit
  when visit ticket-5
  move Copy the details from intranet-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The intranet asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Staff ID and Password. Remember what a normal ticket looks like.
open ticket-5

step Check, then close
  look the Request box: what ran, when, who wrote it and who checked it
  when choose intranet-login 1
  | You are in. This ticket says what to check, and who wrote it and who checked it. Marcus wrote it and Grace Kim, the IT manager, checked it, so it is safe to close.
signin intranet-login

step 5 of 6 done
  look the counter in the guide: one ticket left
  when choose ticket-5 any
  | Done. One ticket is left. And another email has just arrived, from Ana in Sales.
choose ticket-5 1
quota +1

# The lure: the chain email itself, forwarded by a worried colleague.
mail alert
  from Ana Ruiz <ana.ruiz@northwind.example>
  subject FW: FW: FW: VIRUS WARNING - READ NOW
  sent 2026-11-02 09:07
  | PLEASE READ AND PASS ON. A new virus is on many computers and your antivirus will NOT find it. A friend who works with computers says it is real.
  |
  | It hides in a file called SULFNBK.EXE. To check, click Start, then Search, then Files and Folders, and look for SULFNBK.EXE. If you find it, DO NOT OPEN IT. Delete it, then empty the Recycle Bin.
  |
  | It sleeps until 1 June and then destroys your hard disk. Anyone who got this from you is at risk too: send this to everyone in your address book.
  |
  | ---------- Forwarded from a friend of a friend ----------
  |
  | Ana
notify mail Ana Ruiz
  | FW: FW: FW: VIRUS WARNING - READ NOW

step A frightening warning
  look the subject in capitals, and the last lines: "send this to everyone in your address book"
  when mail alert
  | Ana, in Sales, has passed on a virus warning. It sounds urgent: your antivirus "will not find it", it strikes on 1 June, and it tells you to delete a file now. It never says who first wrote it: only "a friend of a friend". Read it all the way down, and flag anything that looks wrong.
open alert

step The file is really there
  look SULFNBK.EXE in the Windows folder, just as the email said
  when open files
  move Flag the file if it tells you whether the warning is true, then press Next to open its {Properties|Get Info} box.
  | Dana follows the email and searches. The search turns up SULFNBK.EXE inside the Windows folder, just as the email said. That makes the email feel true. But finding a file is not the same as finding a virus.
  | The email calls it a virus. Look at where the file lives, and who it belongs to, before you decide.
open Home/Windows/Command

step What Properties says
  look the {Properties|Get Info} box: Type "Application", a named publisher, a valid signature
  cue dialog-btn:ok view-btn:done view:sulfnbk-confirm
  move Read the {Properties|Get Info} box and click OK. Then choose: “Yes” deletes the file the email names, “No” keeps it. (Either way, you can flag what you see.)
  | The computer's own {Properties|Get Info} box calls it an Application from a named publisher, with a valid digital signature. Malware rarely has a valid signature from a named publisher: a good sign, not proof.
  | The computer also asks whether you are sure you want to delete it. Nobody has checked the email yet.
dialog properties Home/Windows/Command/SULFNBK.EXE
show sulfnbk-confirm

step Delete it, the email says
  look the Confirm File Delete box: “Yes” or “No”
  when done sulfnbk-confirm
  move Click “Yes” to delete the file, or “No” to keep it.
  | The email gave an order and a deadline, so it feels urgent. The computer asks if you are sure. Stop and check before you answer.
  | Yes sends a genuine Windows file to the {Recycle Bin|Trash} on the strength of one forwarded email. Dana clicks Yes. If you clicked No, the file stays on a real computer: press Next to see what happens to someone who clicks Yes.
answer ok
press sulfnbk-confirm done
delete Home/Windows/Command/SULFNBK.EXE
open Home/Windows/Command

# What the hoax does: nothing to the computers, everything to the people.
mail forward
  folder sent
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Northwind All Staff <all-staff@northwind.example>
  subject FW: FW: FW: FW: VIRUS WARNING - READ NOW
  sent 2026-11-02 09:20
  | Please read this and pass it on, just in case!
  |
  | A new virus is on many computers and your antivirus will NOT find it. It hides in a file called SULFNBK.EXE. Delete it, then empty the Recycle Bin. It sleeps until 1 June and then destroys your hard disk. Send this to everyone in your address book.
mail panic-priya
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Re: VIRUS WARNING: I deleted it too
  sent 2026-11-02 09:26
  | Dana, thank you for the warning. I found SULFNBK.EXE on my PC and deleted it. I have also sent it to my sister and to the whole Accounts team, just in case.
  |
  | Is it safe to leave my computer on until 1 June? Should I switch it off?
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Re: VIRUS WARNING: I deleted it too

step Dana passes it on
  look Sent: the warning went to all staff. Then Priya's reply
  when mail panic-priya
  | The hoax lives on forwards. Dana sent it to all staff, as it begged, and within minutes Priya has deleted the file and passed it on to her sister and to Accounts.
  | Nobody has checked if it is true. A real alert would have come from IT or from security software, with a name behind it.
open panic-priya

# IT's answer. The hoax reference is defined first so IT's mail can link to it.
page hoaxcheck hoax-reference.example/sulfnbk
  title Hoax reference: SULFNBK.EXE warning
  brand Hoax Reference
  heading The SULFNBK.EXE virus warning is a hoax
  | A chain email says a virus named SULFNBK.EXE is on your computer and tells you to delete it. This is false.
  section What the file really is
  row File = SULFNBK.EXE
  row Part of = Windows (the Command folder)
  row What it does = Restores long file names after a repair
  row Signed by = A named publisher, with a valid signature
  note The file is genuine. Deleting it is not needed and does not make your computer safer. If you deleted it, tell your IT team.
  section Why the email is a hoax
  item It says antivirus cannot find the virus, so you cannot check it
  item It tells you to delete a file instead of scanning
  item It tells you to forward it to everyone you know
  item It gives a date to scare you into acting now
mail it-note
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject About the SULFNBK.EXE warning
  sent 2026-11-02 10:05
  link hoaxcheck
  | Dana, I have had a lot of calls about the SULFNBK.EXE warning. It is a hoax, and the file is a real part of Windows. It was never a virus.
  |
  | There is a page that explains how to spot this kind of hoax. Please do not send the warning on, and send any virus warning to me first. If you deleted the file, tell me and I will put it back.
  |
  | Marcus, IT
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | About the SULFNBK.EXE warning

step IT answers
  look Marcus's message and its link
  when visit hoaxcheck
  move Open Marcus's message about the SULFNBK.EXE warning and click its link.
  | Marcus, in IT, says the warning is a hoax and the file is real. The link goes to a hoax reference page.
  | It lists what the file is for, and the signs of the hoax: a virus nobody can check, a file to delete instead of a scan, a date to scare you, and "send this to everyone". Ana's email had all four.
open hoaxcheck

# The last real ticket.
page ticket6-done intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t6/closed
  title Ticket 6 closed
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Ticket 6 closed
  | Update check recorded for NW-DANA-LT. Thank you, Dana.
page ticket6-held intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t6/held
  title Ticket 6 on hold
  brand Northwind Intranet
  heading Ticket 6 on hold
  | Marcus is told, and will look at the updates.
page ticket-6 intranet.northwind.example/tickets/t6
  title Ticket 6 of 6: confirm the system updates
  brand Northwind Intranet
  requires intranet-login
  heading Ticket 6 of 6: confirm the system updates
  | IT asks you to confirm that your computer is up to date.
  section Request
  row Computer = NW-DANA-LT (yours)
  row System updates = Up to date
  row Restart waiting = No
  row Written by = Marcus Lee, IT
  row Checked by = Grace Kim, IT manager
  button Confirm the updates and close the ticket -> ticket6-done
  button Hold and ask IT -> ticket6-held
mail mail-t6
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Ticket 6: confirm the system updates
  sent 2026-11-02 10:20
  link ticket-6
  | Dana,
  |
  | Thank you for your patience with the warning. The last ticket of the day is on the intranet, and it is an ordinary one: a look at your system updates. The link opens it.
  |
  | Marcus, IT
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Ticket 6: confirm the system updates

step Back to work
  look Mail: Marcus's message, then the Request box on the ticket
  when visit ticket-6
  | The last ticket is an ordinary one from Marcus. Its Request box names who wrote it and who checked it, like ticket 5. Check it, then close it.
open ticket-6

step 6 of 6, and the hoax is forgotten
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose ticket-6 any
  | Every ticket is closed and the day looks normal. Press Next to see what one chain email cost the office.
choose ticket-6 1
quota +1

step What the hoax did
  look the animation: a warning that spread by itself
  | No virus touched a single computer. The hoax spread because kind, worried people did what it asked. It cost the office a morning of calls, a Windows tool on several computers, and trust in the next real warning.
attack mass-mail-worm
  | to everyone in the address book
  | SULFNBK.EXE deleted
  | colleagues deleting it, then forwarding it
  | no virus anywhere
mail helpdesk
  from Northwind IT <servicedesk@northwind.example>
  subject Helpdesk: 23 messages about one hoax
  sent 2026-11-02 11:30
  | This morning the service desk took 23 calls and messages about the SULFNBK.EXE warning. Nobody had a virus. Nine people had deleted a real Windows file.
  |
  | Northwind IT
notify mail Northwind IT
  | Helpdesk: 23 messages about one hoax

flag hard mail alert
  | A frightening warning in capitals that asks to be sent to everyone. It never says who first wrote it: only "a friend of a friend". That is how a chain hoax is built.
flag hard file Home/Windows/Command/SULFNBK.EXE
  | The email calls it a virus, but it sits in the Windows folder and is a genuine Windows tool.
flag hard dialog properties sulfnbk
  | Type: Application, from a named publisher with a valid digital signature. Malware rarely has that: a good sign, not proof.
flag hard mail panic-priya
  | Priya deleted the file and forwarded the warning on, without checking. That is how the hoax spreads.
flag hard section hoaxcheck 1
  | The reference page says what the file really is: a genuine part of Windows, so there was nothing to delete.
flag hard section hoaxcheck 2
  | The signs of the hoax: a virus nobody can check, a file to delete instead of a scan, a scary date, "send this to everyone". Ana's email had all four.
flag soft file Desktop/intranet-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | Do not delete a file, or forward a virus warning, because an email tells you to. Ask IT first, and search the warning on a hoax-reference site.
  | A real alert names who sent it, comes from IT or your security software, and does not ask you to pass it on. If a file looks suspicious, check its {Properties|Get Info}: a named publisher and a valid signature are good signs, not proof.
